Q: Bash tool/function to replace an arbitrary literal string (not regex) in a file/variable A: <p>With GNU <code>awk</code> you cannot use <code>sub</code> or <code>gsub</code> because they perform regular expression matching, while what you want is exact string equality. You can use the <code>index</code> function, instead, and then <code>substr</code> for the replacement:</p>
<pre><code>awk -i inplace -v key=&quot;foo&quot; -v value=&quot;bar&quot; '
{
  while(n = index($0, key)) {
    $0 = substr($0, 1, n-1) value substr($0, n + length(key))
  }
  print
}' file
</code></pre>
<p>Note: the <code>-i inplace</code> option edits in place (equivalent of <code>-i</code> with GNU <code>sed</code>).</p>

Q: Refreshing/Restarting PowerShell session w/out exiting A: <p>Here is an expansion upon <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/42040185/4575793">@Bad's answer</a>. This will now handle ISE (because I use it all the time).</p>
<p>The idea is if you are in ISE, it will restart ISE. If you are in plain PowerShell, it will restart plain PowerShell. <code>Get-PSHostProcessInfo</code> returns information about the current PowerShell session which allows you to determine whether or not you are running ISE or not.</p>
<pre><code>function Restart-PowerShell
{
    if((Get-PSHostProcessInfo).ProcessName.Contains('powershell_ise'))
    {
        Start-Process 'powershell_ise.exe'
    } else {
        Start-Process 'powershell.exe'
    }
    exit
}
</code></pre>

Q: Create an XML file with multiple XMLNS and multiple nesting in SQL A: <p>This works for your scenario, but if that is representative of the problem you are trying to solve is another matter.</p>
<p>I use a couple of statements here, for the nested namespaces. Firstly the inner XML is generated, with the namespace <code>http://www.root.com.au/2013/xml/Tier1</code>, and assigned to a variable. I then generate the outer XML, with the namespace <code>http://www.root.com.au/2013/xml</code>. Note, however, that this will repeat the namespace in the inner element, <code>Tier1</code>; there is probably little you can do about that unless you switch to creating your XML <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/xml/use-raw-mode-with-for-xml?view=sql-server-ver17" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>RAW</code></a>.</p>
<p>You'll note, as well, that I don't use <code>ROOT</code>, so that an attribute can be added it.</p>
<p>This ends up with the following statements:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>DECLARE @XML xml;

WITH XMLNAMESPACES(DEFAULT 'http://www.root.com.au/2013/xml/Tier1')
SELECT @XML = (SELECT (SELECT 1 AS [@Number],
                              1 AS [@Version]
                       FOR XML PATH('Tier4'),ROOT('Tier3'), TYPE)
               FOR XML PATH('Tier2'));

WITH XMLNAMESPACES(DEFAULT 'http://www.root.com.au/2013/xml')
SELECT @XML = (SELECT 'date' AS [@generated],
                      'TypeName' AS [@fileType],
                      '1.0.1' AS [@schemaVersion],
                      (SELECT V.result1 AS [@element1],
                              V.date AS [@date],
                              V.id AS [@id],
                              @XML
                       FROM (VALUES('result1','date','id'))V(result1,date,id)
                       FOR XML PATH('Tier1'), TYPE)
                FOR XML PATH('Root'))

SELECT @XML;
</code></pre>
<p>Which results in the XML:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Root xmlns=&quot;http://www.root.com.au/2013/xml&quot; generated=&quot;date&quot; fileType=&quot;TypeName&quot; schemaVersion=&quot;1.0.1&quot;&gt;
  &lt;Tier1 xmlns=&quot;http://www.root.com.au/2013/xml&quot; element1=&quot;result1&quot; date=&quot;date&quot; id=&quot;id&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Tier2 xmlns=&quot;http://www.root.com.au/2013/xml/Tier1&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Tier3 xmlns=&quot;http://www.root.com.au/2013/xml/Tier1&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Tier4 Number=&quot;1&quot; Version=&quot;1&quot; /&gt;
      &lt;/Tier3&gt;
    &lt;/Tier2&gt;
  &lt;/Tier1&gt;
&lt;/Root&gt;
</code></pre>
